+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,223 @@
+# P3-factor certificates from arXiv:1304.2429
+
+> Status: **locally verified through G8, but not shippable yet**. The required
+> OpenRouter runs reached the service and received only account-level HTTP 403
+> “Key limit exceeded” responses. Those are errors, not failed solver attempts;
+> consequently G9 and the oracle-hardness claim remain pending.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | B — no-tool compression |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite field |
+| computational core | linear algebra on a graph blow-up |
+| certificate form | matrix certificate |
+| native objects | a three-partite `P3` blow-up and its two bipartite super-edges |
+| intended intuition | invariant: neighbor-coordinate sums vary affinely |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## What the family is
+
+Bal, Frieze, Krivelevich, and Loh’s [*Packing tree factors in random and
+pseudo-random graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2429) defines a `T`-factor as
+vertex-disjoint, not necessarily induced copies of `T` covering every vertex.
+Section 3 blows a fixed tree up into equal vertex classes. In the proof of
+Theorem 1, immediately after Lemma 3.4, it observes that one perfect matching
+from every super-edge combines into a `T`-factor.
+
+This family takes `T=P3=L--C--R`. Every class is a copy of `F_q^3`, and the
+solver receives both bipartite adjacency tables. It must give two affine maps
+`x -> A_X x+b_X`, one from `C` to each leaf class. The checker computes both
+determinants modulo `q`, expands the maps, and checks every selected edge.
+Invertibility proves that each map is a perfect matching, so their union is a
+`P3`-factor. The answer is 24 small field elements rather than hundreds of
+vertex IDs.
+
+Generation is by composition, never by solving. For each super-edge the module
+samples an invertible matrix `A` and  `d` translations `b`. Every one of the
+`d` maps is a valid matching and one uniformly chosen map is carried as the
+answer. Plants and decoys are therefore members of the same translation
+distribution. IDs and every irrelevant input order are shuffled.
+
+## Why Track B is the honest claim
+
+Track A would be false. Section 3 obtains the paper’s factors from bipartite
+perfect matchings, and Section 1 explicitly notes that the `K2` case is ordinary
+perfect matching. Theorem 2 is an existence result in the friendly random-graph
+regime `epsilon^4 n p >> log(n)^2`; Section 5 also identifies the opposite easy
+obstruction, isolated vertices below roughly `log(n)/n`.
+
+For this bounded affine certificate language, the disclosed reference algorithm
+chooses possible images of `0,e1,e2,e3`, derives the unique affine map, and checks
+it on all `q^3` center vertices. Its worst-case cost is `O(2 d^4 q^3)`. On the
+shipping instance `q=7,d=11,seed=271828`, it used 531 candidate matrices, 1,151
+edge tests, and 34,524 counted field operations in 0.0095 seconds. Across eight
+audit seeds it solved 8/8 using 531,784 field operations in 0.156 seconds.
+
+The compact route notices that the neighbor multiset has the form
+`{Ax+b : b in B}`. If `S(x)` is its coordinatewise sum, then
+`S(e_j)-S(0)=d A e_j`; since `d` is invertible modulo `q`, four row sums recover
+all columns of `A`, and any neighbor of zero supplies `b`. The conservative
+shipping count is 280 exact operations. That is small enough for the benchmark
+cap but substantially smaller than mechanical affine-image search.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+`make_instance(n=2, degree=5, seed=0)` is hand-scale: each class has eight
+vertices. Its complete rendered instance is:
+
+```text
+AFFINE P3-FACTOR IN A TREE BLOW-UP
+
+The tree P3 is the three-vertex path L--C--R. A P3-factor of a graph
+is a collection of vertex-disjoint (not necessarily induced) copies of
+P3 that covers every graph vertex exactly once.
+
+Here q=2 is prime. Each of L, C, and R has 8=q^3 vertices.
+Every vertex has a displayed integer ID and an intrinsic coordinate
+(x0,x1,x2) in F_q^3; all coordinate arithmetic below is modulo q.
+Edges exist only on the two displayed bipartite super-edges C--L and
+C--R. Every adjacency row has exactly 5 distinct neighbors.
+
+Your certificate must give, independently for X=L and X=R, an affine
+map f_X(x)=A_X*x+b_X over F_q. A_X must be an invertible 3x3 matrix
+and b_X a length-3 vector. For every center coordinate x, the graph
+must contain the edge from that center vertex to the X-vertex with
+coordinate f_X(x). Invertibility makes each f_X a bijection, so the two
+matchings then combine into a P3-factor with center class C.
+
+Vertex tables (input order has no significance):
+  C vertices as ID:coordinate
+  19:(0,0,1) 18:(1,1,1) 13:(0,0,0) 9:(1,1,0) 0:(0,1,1) 16:(0,1,0) 3:(1,0,1) 7:(1,0,0)
+  L vertices as ID:coordinate
+  14:(0,1,1) 20:(0,0,1) 2:(1,0,0) 23:(1,1,0) 17:(0,1,0) 4:(0,0,0) 10:(1,0,1) 1:(1,1,1)
+  R vertices as ID:coordinate
+  21:(0,0,0) 12:(1,1,0) 6:(1,0,1) 8:(1,0,0) 22:(1,1,1) 11:(0,1,1) 5:(0,0,1) 15:(0,1,0)
+
+Super-edge adjacency; neighbor order and row order have no significance:
+  C--R
+    center 3 -> 8,21,6,11,22
+    center 7 -> 5,15,12,6,22
+    center 0 -> 11,8,21,5,22
+    center 13 -> 12,22,11,21,8
+    center 16 -> 5,11,15,6,12
+    center 18 -> 5,15,21,6,12
+    center 9 -> 11,22,21,15,8
+    center 19 -> 8,12,5,6,15
+  C--L
+    center 3 -> 23,14,2,17,4
+    center 9 -> 23,17,20,4,2
+    center 19 -> 1,17,10,20,14
+    center 7 -> 14,20,2,1,10
+    center 16 -> 14,20,1,10,4
+    center 0 -> 4,23,1,2,17
+    center 18 -> 23,10,20,1,14
+    center 13 -> 10,2,17,4,23
+
+Find any certificate satisfying these conditions. Coefficients must be
+JSON integers in the inclusive range 0..1. Matrix row/column order
+and offset coordinate order are x0,x1,x2. The keys must be exactly L and R.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON object
+with the exact shape shown below (the numbers shown are syntax only):
+<answer>{"L":{"matrix":[[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]],"offset":[0,0,0]},"R":{"matrix":[[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]],"offset":[0,0,0]}}</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The carried answer is:
+
+```json
+{"L":{"matrix":[[0,1,1],[0,0,1],[1,1,1]],"offset":[1,1,0]},"R":{"matrix":[[0,1,0],[0,0,1],[1,1,0]],"offset":[1,1,1]}}
+```
+
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Removing the last
+left-offset coordinate returns `(False, "L offset must have length 3")`. A
+person can solve this demo by enumerating affine images or by using the row-sum
+invariant.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | field `q` | vertices per class `q^3` | degree `d` | affine coefficients | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 2 | 8 | 5 | 24 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 3 | 27 | 5 | 24 | oracle call blocked before scoring |
+| medium | 5 | 125 | 9 | 24 | not reached |
+| hard | 7 | 343 | 11 | 24 | provisional shipping preset; local gates pass |
+
+`escalate()` increases the field order to the next prime beyond twice the old
+one while keeping the 24-entry answer and degree fixed. Thus it grows the graph
+and validation haystack without lengthening the witness.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted certificates verified across all presets |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware random affine bijections verified |
+| G5 | pass | exactly 121 valid of 134,280,722,033,048,457,216 certificates; density `9.01e-19`; reference cost recorded above |
+| G6 | pass | four tool-free attacks each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | field 7 grew to 17 (343 to 4,913 vertices/class) and the carried answer verified |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 ID/order/affine/reflection invariance checks and carried witnesses; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **pending/fail** | size caps pass, but no oracle attempt was scored because the account returned HTTP 403 |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare script made four redraws at `easy`; all were HTTP 403 account-limit
+errors, so `solved=error` and the harness correctly stopped without a verdict.
+Seeds were 335366019, 1964402688, 2064495695, and 1532877293. The models drawn
+were Grok 4.6 and Claude Sonnet 5. No preset has been shown hardened.
+
+| G9 arm | scored solved / attempts | script records | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 errors | unknown |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 errors | G9(b) not established |
+| placebo | 0 / 0 | 4 errors | diagnostic unavailable |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined. The worst-case serialized answer is 146
+characters, 37 estimated tokens, and 24 atomic field elements. The intended
+route is 280 exact operations. These satisfy G9(c), but they do not substitute
+for the missing G9(b) evidence.
+
+## How to use it
+
+```python
+from gen_1304_2429 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+print(verify(inst, inst["answer"]))  # (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After OpenRouter quota is restored, rerun the bare harness and both G9 scratch
+arms, update `G9_ORACLE_RESULTS`, and rerun `selftest()` before emission:
+
+```bash
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_1304_2429.py
+bash ../../scripts/emit.sh 1304.2429
+```
+
+The module uses only the standard library; its optional `gvlib` import is not
+required.
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a native tree-blow-up/factor problem, but the finite-field coordinates
+and affine answer compression are generator structure, not a distribution
+studied by the paper. It exercises the matching-composition step of Section 3,
+not the full approximate packing theorem and not `G_{n,p}` pseudorandomness.
+
+The exact G5 density is relative to the declared prior of two uniformly random
+affine bijections. It is not a density among arbitrary explicit perfect
+matchings, nor a Bayesian estimate for a solver exploiting adjacency. The
+attack panel tests degree/frequency outliers, lexicographic basis choices, 256
+random basis-image restarts, and the identity-matrix ansatz. It does not test
+all algebraic-recovery methods, SAT/ILP encodings, or learned recognition. In
+fact the neighbor-sum decoder deliberately solves every instance efficiently;
+that is the Track B compact route. A graph matcher also finds an explicit
+`P3`-factor quickly, although the required output is the affine coefficient
+compression rather than a long edge list. Most importantly, no model-hardness
+claim is justified until the quota failure is cleared and all three transcript
+arms contain scored attempts.
