## What the family is

Bal, Frieze, Krivelevich, and Loh’s [*Packing tree factors in random and
pseudo-random graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2429) defines a `T`-factor as
vertex-disjoint, not necessarily induced copies of `T` covering every vertex.
Section 3 blows a fixed tree up into equal vertex classes. In the proof of
Theorem 1, immediately after Lemma 3.4, it observes that one perfect matching
from every super-edge combines into a `T`-factor.

This family takes `T=P3=L--C--R`. Every class is a copy of `F_q^3`, and the
solver receives both bipartite adjacency tables. It must give two affine maps
`x -> A_X x+b_X`, one from `C` to each leaf class. The checker computes both
determinants modulo `q`, expands the maps, and checks every selected edge.
Invertibility proves that each map is a perfect matching, so their union is a
`P3`-factor. The answer is 24 small field elements rather than hundreds of
vertex IDs.

Generation is by composition, never by solving. For each super-edge the module
samples an invertible matrix `A` and  `d` translations `b`. Every one of the
`d` maps is a valid matching and one uniformly chosen map is carried as the
answer. Plants and decoys are therefore members of the same translation
distribution. IDs and every irrelevant input order are shuffled.

## Why Track B is the honest claim

Track A would be false. Section 3 obtains the paper’s factors from bipartite
perfect matchings, and Section 1 explicitly notes that the `K2` case is ordinary
perfect matching. Theorem 2 is an existence result in the friendly random-graph
regime `epsilon^4 n p >> log(n)^2`; Section 5 also identifies the opposite easy
obstruction, isolated vertices below roughly `log(n)/n`.

For this bounded affine certificate language, the disclosed reference algorithm
chooses possible images of `0,e1,e2,e3`, derives the unique affine map, and checks
it on all `q^3` center vertices. Its worst-case cost is `O(2 d^4 q^3)`. On the
shipping instance `q=7,d=11,seed=271828`, it used 531 candidate matrices, 1,151
edge tests, and 34,524 counted field operations in 0.0095 seconds. Across eight
audit seeds it solved 8/8 using 531,784 field operations in 0.156 seconds.

The compact route notices that the neighbor multiset has the form
`{Ax+b : b in B}`. If `S(x)` is its coordinatewise sum, then
`S(e_j)-S(0)=d A e_j`; since `d` is invertible modulo `q`, four row sums recover
all columns of `A`, and any neighbor of zero supplies `b`. The conservative
shipping count is 280 exact operations. That is small enough for the benchmark
cap but substantially smaller than mechanical affine-image search.

