-## What the family is
+## What the family asks
 
-Bal, Frieze, Krivelevich, and Loh’s [*Packing tree factors in random and
-pseudo-random graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2429) defines a `T`-factor as
-vertex-disjoint, not necessarily induced copies of `T` covering every vertex.
-Section 3 blows a tree up into equal vertex classes. In the proof of Theorem 1,
-immediately after Lemma 3.4, one perfect matching from each super-edge is united
-to form a `T`-factor.
+[Bal, Frieze, Krivelevich, and Loh, *Packing tree factors in random and pseudo-random graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2429) defines an (H)-factor as vertex-disjoint copies of (H) covering every vertex and notes in Section 1 that a (K_2)-factor is a perfect matching. The generated instance is a bipartite graph whose two sides are (mathbb F_p). A solver must give (k) degree-one polynomials (f_i(x)=a_i x+b_i) whose graphs are pairwise edge-disjoint perfect matchings in the displayed graph.
 
-This family uses `T=P3=L--C--R`. Each class is `F_q^3`. Both displayed
-super-edges are unions of `d` affine perfect matchings `x -> A*x+b` with a
-common invertible matrix `A`. The answer is `A` plus an offset appearing on
-both sides. The checker recomputes every residual set
-`{y-A*x : y adjacent to x}` exactly. Constant residual sets decompose both
-super-edges into matchings; pairing them gives `d` edge-disjoint `P3`-factors,
-and the common offset identifies one aligned factor. Generation samples `A`
-and the translation sets first, so no generated instance is ever solved to
-obtain its certificate.
+The answer is a symbolic packing, not a list of its (kp) edges. `verify` expands every polynomial over all (p) residues, checks every edge, proves that each right side is covered once, and checks edge-disjointness. It accepts every valid list of affine factors and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-## Why Track B is the honest claim
+## Why this is Track B
 
-Track A would be false. Section 1 identifies the `K2` case with ordinary
-perfect matching, and Section 3 obtains tree factors from bipartite perfect
-matchings. Lemma 3.4 handles regular pseudo-random pairs; Lemma 4.1 handles
-random bipartite pairs. Theorem 2 assumes `epsilon^4 n p >> log(n)^2`, while
-Section 5 notes the opposite obstruction: below roughly `log(n)/n`, isolated
-vertices prevent even one factor.
+The paper proves existence rather than computational hardness. In its Section 3 proof of Theorem 1, one perfect matching is chosen on every super-edge and their union is a tree factor; Lemma 3.4 supplies large collections of edge-disjoint perfect matchings. The random-graph analogues are Theorems 2 and 3. None licenses a Track A distributional-hardness claim, and ordinary bipartite matching is polynomial-time.
 
